 `CustomerRepository` deliberately behaves like a real paged backend: it refuses
 to return more than `MAX_PAGE_SIZE` (200) rows in a single call. Callers page.

+## Filtering
+
+The list can be narrowed by name and by status. Both halves of the filter are
+applied by the backend — `CustomerRepository` takes a `CustomerFilter` and serves
+the filtered page and the filtered count — so the grid stays lazily loaded and
+only ever holds one page. The name filter matches customers whose first or last
+name contains every entered term, ignoring case and accents, so `makinen` finds
+`Mäkinen`.
+
+The filter lives in the view's URL as the `name` and `status` query parameters,
+for example `/?name=vir&status=ACTIVE,CLOSED`: opening such a URL applies the
+filter and fills in the fields, and changing the filter rewrites the address bar
+(replacing the history entry rather than pushing one per keystroke). Typing is
+debounced, so a typed name costs the backend one query, not one per letter.
